1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,076
This is ResearchAgent: research with a wallet.

2
00:00:03,376 --> 00:00:08,467
It pays writers for the articles it's missing,
inside your budget, on the XRP Ledger.

3
00:00:09,967 --> 00:00:12,886
Open the link, ask a question, set a budget.

4
00:00:23,067 --> 00:00:28,723
A five-second plan shows what it will do: free
sources first, then at most one article per round.

5
00:00:29,067 --> 00:00:32,851
A model recommends what to buy, but
only policy code can pay.

6
00:00:33,151 --> 00:00:37,653
Each payment sits in a native XRPL escrow,
and the writer is paid by publishing the

7
00:00:37,653 --> 00:00:39,373
article's key on the ledger.

8
00:00:56,048 --> 00:00:58,761
Here's the key, on the ledger, in the fulfilment.

9
00:00:59,061 --> 00:01:00,891
Delivery and payment are one event.

10
00:01:01,248 --> 00:01:06,089
This article broke its promise, so the writer
signed a refund and its trust score dropped.

11
00:01:06,448 --> 00:01:10,893
A real writer could refuse; then the buyer
keeps a fraud proof anyone can check.

12
00:01:12,393 --> 00:01:13,605
Any agent can use it.

13
00:01:13,905 --> 00:01:18,340
Claude Code adds it with one line, and the
spending caps live in the environment, where

14
00:01:18,340 --> 00:01:19,914
the model can't raise them.

15
00:01:20,214 --> 00:01:25,381
Ask about XRPL and it buys from our own build
notes, labelled as ours, and answers with

16
00:01:25,381 --> 00:01:27,215
citations and ledger links.

17
00:01:40,368 --> 00:01:41,213
Don't trust us.

18
00:01:41,513 --> 00:01:45,885
A Python verifier, written separately from
our TypeScript one, re-checks every

19
00:01:45,885 --> 00:01:47,602
receipt against the ledger.

20
00:01:52,368 --> 00:01:56,150
Our ledger page re-validates every recorded
transaction from your browser.

21
00:01:59,299 --> 00:02:02,700
For builders: one line, no clone, no account.

22
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:08,994
It buys, catches a broken promise, gets refunded,
re-buys, anchors the answer and verifies all six

23
00:02:08,994 --> 00:02:11,185
transactions against the ledger.

24
00:02:14,299 --> 00:02:19,382
We wrote down what fought us: seven ranked findings,
each with a script that reproduces it.

25
00:02:19,699 --> 00:02:26,455
The top one: the RLUSD issuer lacks the flag token
escrow needs, so we use a labelled Testnet token.

26
00:02:26,755 --> 00:02:28,914
Mainnet is a checklist, not a claim.

27
00:02:30,114 --> 00:02:31,028
ResearchAgent.

28
00:02:31,328 --> 00:02:36,121
An open market where agents pay the people who
know, and the ledger keeps everyone honest.

29
00:02:36,421 --> 00:02:36,996
Thank you.
